      "id": "msg-1793170293972-5",
      "role": "user",
      "content": "P8 native acceptance: explain bounded queues in twelve short numbered points. Do not use tools, read files, or change anything. End with P8-CHAT-DONE.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAtMs": 1793170293972
    },
    {
      "id": "msg-1793170293969-4",
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "1. A bounded queue holds a fixed maximum number of items.\n2. Producers add items; consumers remove them.\n3. Its capacity limits how many items can wait.\n4. When full, it cannot accept items normally.\n5. Producers may block until space becomes available.\n6. Alternatively, additions may fail or time out.\n7. Some queues drop items according to a defined policy.\n8. These limits help prevent uncontrolled memory growth.\n9. Blocking creates backpressure, slowing producers.\n10. Larger queues absorb bursts but can increase waiting time.\n11. Thread-safe queues coordinate concurrent producers and consumers.\n12. Monitor queue depth, wait times, and dropped items.\n\nP8-CHAT-DONE",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAtMs": 1793170293973
    },
